WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:01.218
Welcome.

2
00:00:01.498 --> 00:00:06.763
In this short video, we look at what
Responsible Research and Innovation, or RRI,

3
00:00:06.763 --> 00:00:11.247
means, why it has become so important,
and where the idea comes from.

4
00:00:12.447 --> 00:00:17.205
Science and technology are transforming
society at an unprecedented pace.

5
00:00:17.485 --> 00:00:22.429
New tools reshape how we live, work and
make decisions, often faster than our

6
00:00:22.429 --> 00:00:23.906
institutions can adapt.

7
00:00:25.106 --> 00:00:27.090
We have also learned some hard lessons.

8
00:00:27.370 --> 00:00:32.641
Controversies over genetically modified
crops, nuclear power and, more recently,

9
00:00:32.641 --> 00:00:37.846
smart meters, show what happens when
society is engaged too late, after the key

10
00:00:37.846 --> 00:00:40.021
decisions have already been made.

11
00:00:41.221 --> 00:00:46.413
At the same time, grand challenges such as
climate change, health and sustainability

12
00:00:46.413 --> 00:00:50.863
call for research that is aimed
squarely at what society actually needs.

13
00:00:52.063 --> 00:00:53.941
This is where RRI comes in.

14
00:00:54.221 --> 00:00:56.375
It is not just about avoiding harm.

15
00:00:56.655 --> 00:01:01.664
It is about actively shaping science and
innovation for ethical, inclusive and

16
00:01:01.664 --> 00:01:03.013
sustainable outcomes.

17
00:01:04.213 --> 00:01:07.605
A widely used definition
comes from René von Schomberg.

18
00:01:07.885 --> 00:01:13.446
He describes RRI as a transparent, interactive
process, by which societal actors and

19
00:01:13.446 --> 00:01:18.676
innovators become mutually responsive to
each other, with a view to the ethical

20
00:01:18.676 --> 00:01:24.039
acceptability, sustainability and societal
desirability of the innovation process

21
00:01:24.039 --> 00:01:25.165
and its products.

22
00:01:26.365 --> 00:01:31.355
In plain terms, it marks a shift from
science in society, to science for and with

23
00:01:31.355 --> 00:01:31.847
society.

24
00:01:32.127 --> 00:01:36.890
Public values steer research from the
start, not as an afterthought, and the aim

25
00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:38.677
goes beyond simple compliance.

26
00:01:39.877 --> 00:01:45.693
In European policy, the Commission framed
RRI around six keys: public engagement,

27
00:01:45.693 --> 00:01:50.863
open access, gender equality, science
education, ethics, and governance.

28
00:01:51.143 --> 00:01:55.666
These became cross-cutting themes
of the Horizon 2020 programme.

29
00:01:56.866 --> 00:02:02.479
Scholars such as Stilgoe, Owen and Macnaghten
distilled RRI into four interlinked

30
00:02:02.479 --> 00:02:07.191
dimensions: anticipation, inclusion,
reflexivity and responsiveness.

31
00:02:07.471 --> 00:02:10.095
The next video explores
each of them in practice.

32
00:02:11.295 --> 00:02:12.639
Thank you for watching.

33
00:02:12.919 --> 00:02:16.439
Continue with the module to
put these ideas into practice.
